  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2022;	Workers organizations in Indonesia in the Garments, Palm Oil and Fisheries sectors conducted an in-depth analysis of their grievance management systems and service provision methodologies and agreed to create a digital platform for addressing grievances and coordination of services. By the end of 2023, the pilot of this platform will be adopted by the national confederations as part of expanding their service provision to workers in the aforementioned sectors.   The digital grievance management tools enable the workers&apos; organizations to manage grievances, its cases load, coordination between workers and management for amicable dialogue and coordination of settlement of conciliatory disputes through the Ministry of Manpower
&#x2022;	Enhanced participation of the women leaders within the union organizations. 
&#x2022;	The ability of trade union members to improve working conditions and conduct a social dialogue with employers in the palm oil sector has strengthened.
 &#x2022;	The ILO provided technical support through online and in-person meetings, research documentation, technical reviews of documentation, coordination amongst WO and software developers on the design of the proposed system by the workers
&#x2022;	The ILO has conducted a study of trade unions&#x2019; grievance mechanisms and information systems. The ILO also has prepared to start deploying training programmes targeting unaffiliated trade unions.
The study found that the workers are generally very satisfied with the results of federations resolving their grievances, but that the federations&#x2019; focus on worker advocacy and outcomes and their resource limitations may be currently working at the cost of creating systems that allow the trade union federations to work more efficiently and effectively overall.
&#x2022;	The ILO completed women&apos;s leadership training for union partners in May, June, and July 2023. The training successfully motivated the women leaders to commit to fight for several significant issues, such as sexual harassment, and improvement of participation in industrial training.
&#x2022;	The ILO has facilitated the establishment of JAPBUSI (Trade Union Network). It consists of 10 federations of oil palm plantation workers. From the union&apos;s perspective, efforts to fight for decent work are not easy. JAPBUSI has long wanted bipartite cooperation with groups of oil palm employers to be formal or binding. 
JAPBUSI collaborated with palm oil employers (GAPKI) in a forum called JAGASAWITAN on 15 February 2023. This bipartite collaboration seeks to realize sustainable palm oil that aims to create decent work in all supply chains. The collaboration includes industrial relations settlement agreements, respect for freedom of association, labour agreement compliance, and occupational safety and health by improving working conditions in all plantation areas of GAPKI members.</narrative>
   </description>
